[385,3,0,1877,"\"Strawberries. All ripe!\"","A street seller with a donkey cart calls out her berries on a London street. John Thomson and the writer Adolphe Smith made Street Life in London to show the people who earned a living on the streets.","How would you advertise fresh strawberries if you were selling them in the street?"],
[386,3,0,1877,"The \"Crawlers\"","\"Crawlers\" was a name for the poorest people of London, often elderly women who had no work and wandered the streets. Thomson's photographs made readers see the people behind the word.","Why might a picture change how you feel about someone?"],
[388,3,0,1877,"When the Day's Work Is Done","Henry Peach Robinson arranged this cozy cottage scene with models. A family gathers around a table in warm light at the end of a working day, like a painting made with a camera.","What do you think each person is doing after a long day?"],
[389,3,0,1877,"London Nomads","Some families lived and traveled in wooden wagons, moving through London and the English countryside. John Thomson photographed them in front of their caravan for Street Life in London.","What would it be like to live in a wagon?"],
[391,3,0,1877,"Covent Garden Labourers","Porters in London's Covent Garden market carried heavy baskets of fruit and vegetables on their heads. Thomson photographed them taking a break.","How heavy do you think the baskets were?"],
[392,7,0,1878,"Thomas Edison and His Phonograph","Thomas Edison invented the phonograph, a machine that could record and play back sound, in 1877. This photograph was taken in Mathew Brady's Washington studio after Edison stayed up all night showing it off.","What would you record on a phonograph?"],
[396,3,0,1878,"In the Haysel","Peter Henry Emerson photographed farm life in Norfolk, England, with a cart piled with hay beside the trees. He wanted photographs that looked the way the human eye sees, a bit soft around the edges.","What time of year do you think it is?"],
[398,7,0,1878,"Sallie Gardner at a Gallop","Eadweard Muybridge lined up cameras along a racetrack so a galloping horse tripped the shutters one after another. The twelve pictures show the horse, Sallie Gardner, with all four hooves off the ground at once.","Look at the horse's legs. When are all four hooves off the ground?"],
[399,7,0,1878,"The Horse in Motion","Leland Stanford, a rich California governor, bet that a galloping horse lifts all four hooves off the ground. Muybridge proved him right with his photographs, and they also helped inspire the first movies.","Do the horse's legs look the way painters usually showed them?"],
[401,2,0,1878,"U.S. Appraiser's Store, San Francisco","Eadweard Muybridge photographed San Francisco's big new buildings from above. The wide streets and awnings show a city growing fast after the Gold Rush.","What do you see on the street below?"],
[402,0,0,1879,"Spotted Fawn, Cheyenne Bride","L. A. Huffman lived in Miles City, Montana, and photographed Native people, cowboys and the great plains. This portrait of a Cheyenne woman, her braids neatly hanging, was made when the old ways of life on the plains were changing quickly.","What do you notice about her clothes and hair?"],
[403,0,0,1845,"James Nasmyth","James Nasmyth was a Scottish engineer who invented the steam hammer and made careful drawings of the Moon. Hill and Adamson photographed him resting his head on his hand, as if deep in thought.","What might an inventor be thinking about?"],
[405,0,0,1907,"On the House Boat","Edward Steichen was one of the first artists to try Autochrome, the first practical color process. Women in light summer dresses lounge on a veranda, and the soft colors look like an Impressionist painting.","What colors do you notice? How does it feel to see old photographs in color?"],
[406,0,0,1879,"Frederick Douglass","Frederick Douglass was a leader in the fight to end slavery and win equal rights. In this portrait taken around 1879, he is in his sixties with a full white beard and a calm gaze.","What does his steady look tell you?"],
[411,5,1,1880,"Plaster Cast of a Body, Pompeii","When Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 AD, ash buried the city of Pompeii. Later, archaeologists poured plaster into the hollows left by bodies, creating casts like this one. Giorgio Sommer photographed them for visitors.","How can an empty space in ash tell us what a person looked like?"],
[413,2,0,1900,"Gloucester Cathedral, South Transept","Frederick H. Evans photographed English cathedrals, waiting hours for the light to be just right. He printed on platinum paper, which gave soft, silvery grays, and refused to touch up his pictures.","How does the light make the stone look?"],
[421,3,0,1888,"A Stiff Pull","A farmer guides two horses pulling a plow across a big field under a gray sky. Peter Henry Emerson wanted to show the strength and beauty of working life in the English countryside.","How do you think the farmer and horses feel at the end of the day?"],
[422,3,0,1888,"A Toad in the Path","Two boys stop on a path in the marshes of Norfolk to look at a toad. Emerson's photographs from East Anglia caught everyday moments of country life.","What would you do if you found a toad on your path?"],
[424,2,0,1883,"The Head of the Statue of Liberty","In Paris, the giant head of the Statue of Liberty stood in a yard while the rest of the statue was built. The statue was later taken apart and shipped to New York in crates.","How tall are the people standing beside the head?"],
[426,5,0,1885,"The Kailasa Temple, Ellora","The Kailasa temple in India was carved out of a single hillside of rock, from the top down. Raja Deen Dayal photographed its carved columns and towers.","How could people carve a whole temple out of one rock?"],
[429,3,0,1886,"Coming Home from the Marshes","Men carry bundles of reeds home across the marshes of the Norfolk Broads. Emerson made soft-focus pictures like this to show the beauty of a vanishing way of life.","What did people use reeds for?"],
[431,3,0,1886,"Gathering Water-Lilies","Two people gather water lilies from a small boat among the reeds of the Norfolk Broads. Emerson's pictures of this watery landscape became famous and made photographers think about how the eye really sees.","What do you think they will do with the lilies?"],
